            "Assisting a patient to eat is a clinical procedure, not a chore to be rushed between drug rounds. The patient is offered the toilet and hand hygiene, has mouth care and the dentures in and glasses on, is sat <strong>upright at ninety degrees</strong> in the chair or the bed with the head slightly forward and the chin tucked, and the tray is placed where it can be seen and reached. The nurse <strong>sits at eye level</strong>, tells the patient what is on the plate, offers choice and the patient's own pace, gives <strong>small mouthfuls</strong> — about half a teaspoon — placed on the stronger side of the mouth after a stroke, waits for each swallow by watching the larynx rise and the mouth empty, alternates solids with sips of fluid, does not talk to the patient while his mouth is full or to a colleague while feeding him, and stops at the first sign of trouble. Afterwards the mouth is checked for pocketed food, the patient stays upright for at least thirty minutes, and the <strong>amount eaten</strong> — a quarter, half, all — is recorded on the food chart, not 'diet taken'. A blind patient is told the plate as a clock face; a patient with a weak arm is given adapted cutlery and a plate guard; the patient who can feed herself but slowly is given the time and not fed to save it.",
