            "A nasogastric tube is passed for <strong>feeding</strong> and giving medicines to the patient who cannot swallow safely or enough, for <strong>decompression</strong> of the stomach in obstruction, ileus and after some abdominal surgery, for <strong>aspiration</strong> of stomach contents in poisoning or bleeding, and for <strong>lavage</strong>; fine-bore tubes are for feeding, wide-bore (Ryle's) tubes for drainage. It is contraindicated without medical direction in facial and base-of-skull fractures (the tube can enter the cranium), recent nasal or oesophageal surgery, oesophageal varices and strictures, and bleeding disorders. The procedure: explain and agree a signal to stop; sit the patient <strong>upright with the chin slightly tucked</strong>; check the nostrils and choose the clearer; <strong>measure NEX</strong> — nose tip to earlobe to xiphisternum — and mark the tube; lubricate the tip with water-soluble jelly; pass the tube along the floor of the nose aiming <strong>backwards</strong>, not upwards; when it reaches the throat ask the patient to <strong>sip water and swallow</strong> and advance with each swallow to the mark; <strong>stop and withdraw</strong> if there is coughing, choking, cyanosis, respiratory distress or the tube coils in the mouth; secure it to the nose and the cheek with tape that does not press on the nostril; and <strong>confirm the position</strong> before anything goes down it. In the unconscious patient the tube is passed with the head flexed forward and the airway protected, and the risk of misplacement is higher.",
