          heading: "Normal elimination and what alters it",
          paragraphs: [
            "Food enters the large intestine as liquid chyme, water and electrolytes are absorbed as it passes through the colon over twelve to forty-eight hours, and the <strong>mass movements</strong> that follow a meal — the gastrocolic reflex, strongest after breakfast — push formed stool into the rectum, where distension triggers the <strong>defaecation reflex</strong>: the internal sphincter relaxes involuntarily, and the external sphincter is relaxed voluntarily when the person chooses, with the help of the <strong>Valsalva manoeuvre</strong> and the squatting position that straightens the anorectal angle. Normal frequency ranges from three times a day to three times a week; normal stool is brown from bile pigments, soft, formed and passed without straining — type 3 or 4 on the <strong>Bristol stool chart</strong>, which runs from type 1 (separate hard lumps) through type 4 (smooth soft sausage) to type 7 (entirely liquid) and gives the nurse and the patient a common language. Elimination is altered by age (the infant's reflex emptying; the older person's slower transit, weaker muscles and polypharmacy), diet (fibre and fluid make bulk; a low-fibre diet of rice and bread makes constipation), activity, the psychological state and the lack of privacy, pregnancy, pain (a haemorrhoid or a perineal wound makes people hold on), anaesthesia and surgery (paralytic ileus for a day or three), drugs (opioids, iron, anticholinergics, antacids containing aluminium and calcium cause constipation; antibiotics, magnesium antacids, laxatives and metformin cause diarrhoea) and the diagnostic tests that empty or fill the bowel.",
            "The <strong>assessment</strong> is a history — usual pattern, last stool, its form on the Bristol chart, straining, pain, blood or mucus, laxative use, diet and fluid, mobility, drugs, and what has changed — an inspection of the abdomen for distension and of the stool itself, auscultation for bowel sounds (normal 5–30 a minute; absent after five minutes; high-pitched tinkling in obstruction), palpation for faecal masses in the left iliac fossa, and, when impaction is suspected and the policy allows, a <strong>digital rectal examination</strong> with consent, lubricated glove and the patient in the left lateral position, feeling for hard stool, a ballooned empty rectum, a mass or blood on the glove. The nurse records the stool on the chart by date, time, Bristol type and amount, not as 'bowels opened'.",
          ],
